NAHANT, Aug. 10.—The two New York men, Clarence Hobart and J. L. Hall, were both successful in their matches to-day in the continuation of the Nahant invitation tennis Tournament. Hobart tonight stands as in head of the contestants, with four victories to his credit and no defeats. This morning he defeated F. S. Sears after the latter had won the first set, and later in the day he beat Quincy A. Shaw, Jr., easily. Hobart's important match will probably be on Friday against T. J. Hovey. If he wins he takes his prize.

The great surprise of the day's play was the defeat of Hovey by Hall in two straight sets. Two weeks ago Hovey defeated Hall in a close two-set match for the New England championship between these players, each having previously won two. His swift and sure volley was an especial feature of his game, though the quick rallies were decided in his favor; the court of to-day was greatly to some advantage. Very good playing was done by G. Hubbard, the Californian, who won glory on the Pacific slope by defeating the Western champion, J. J. Chase. The scores were as follows:

second set—Hobart, New York, beat Philip York, Boston, 6-0, 6-0;
first set—Hall, New York, beat Hovey, Boston, 6-4, 6-2;
unfinished: Clarence Hobart, beat M. Chase, 6-4, 6-2;
G. Hubbard beat J. J. Chase, 6-0, 6-3.

Standing of the contestants: Clarence Hobart, won 4, lost 0; J. L. Hall, won 3, lost 1; J. J. Chase, won 0, lost 3; G. Hubbard, won 3, lost 0; Quincy A. Shaw, Jr., won 1, lost 3; F. S. Sears, won 1, lost 4.
